---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { READ_ROWS } from '../../lib/reference/tables';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Where to read each number"
  description="Which VASP output file holds each result, what to grep for, and which reader on this site parses it."
>
  <div class="container ref-page" data-pagefind-body data-pagefind-meta="module:Quick reference">
    <h1>Where to read each number</h1>
    <p>
      A finished run leaves a dozen files. This table says which one holds each number and which line to look
      for. It merges the "Where do I find ..." table of the Field Handbook (
      <a href="/references/#S-041">S-041</a>) with the "Where to read it" table of the Workflow Handbook (
      <a href="/references/#S-042">S-042</a>). The last column names the reader on this site that parses the
      file, so you can drop the file in and read the number in your browser; nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
    <div class="ref-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Where to read each number" tabindex="0">
      <table data-testid="read-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">You want</th>
            <th scope="col">Look in</th>
            <th scope="col">Reader on this site</th>
            <th scope="col">Source</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {READ_ROWS.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{r.quantity}</th>
              <td>{r.where}</td>
              <td>
                {r.link ? <a href={r.link.href}>{r.link.label}</a> : <span>Read it by eye</span>}
                {r.parser && (
                  <>
                    <br />
                    <code>
                      {r.parser.fn}().{r.parser.field}
                    </code>
                  </>
                )}
              </td>
              <td>{r.sources.join(', ')}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Why it matters: the same name can mean different numbers. "Energy" in OSZICAR is <code>F</code> (with
      the smearing entropy) and <code>E0</code> (extrapolated to zero smearing); use one of them consistently.
      The Fermi level must come from the self-consistent run, not from a band run.
    </p>
    <p>
      See also <a href="/reference/units/">units and conversions</a> and the
      <a href="/reference/checklist/">pre-submit checklist</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
